            <ArticleTitle>Clearing the smoke around medical marijuana.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>769-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/clpt.2011.241</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The hazy world of &quot;medical marijuana&quot; continues to cry out for clear data on which to base medical decision making and rational policy design. In this issue of Clinical Pharmacology &amp; Therapeutics, Abrams and colleagues report that vaporized cannabis does not meaningfully affect opioid plasma levels and may even augment the efficacy of oxycodone and morphine in patients with chronic non-cancer pain. This Commentary considers the implications of this work for clinical practice and further research initiatives.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
